import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/*
We have cylinders (circles in cross-section) with given diameters d[i], in fixed order.
All cylinders lie between two parallel walls. We want the minimum wall distance H
such that the circles can be arranged, preserving order, without overlap.

Let wall distance be H. A circle of radius r can fit only if H >= 2r.
Its center must lie on the horizontal line segment between the walls:
y in [r, H-r].

For two consecutive circles i,j with radii r1,r2 and center vertical coordinates y1,y2,
their horizontal distance must satisfy:
dx^2 + (y1-y2)^2 >= (r1+r2)^2
So the minimum possible horizontal separation for chosen y's is
sqrt((r1+r2)^2 - (y1-y2)^2), minimized by maximizing |y1-y2|.
Since y1,y2 can be chosen independently in [r1,H-r1], [r2,H-r2],
the maximum possible |y1-y2| is H - r1 - r2 (when positive; and if H < r1+r2,
intervals overlap and max is smaller, but formula below still yields correct piecewise result).

Thus minimum separation between consecutive circles is:
if H >= r1+r2: 2*sqrt(r1*r2)
else: sqrt((r1+r2)^2 - (H-r1-r2)^2)

For a feasible arrangement preserving order, total horizontal span can always be compressed
to the sum of these minimum consecutive separations, and there is no horizontal bound.
Hence feasibility depends only on all circles fitting individually: H >= max diameter.
But the actual problem asks minimum distance between walls when cylinders are squeezed together.
This classic interpretation means the cylinders form a chain touching consecutively and walls,
and we seek the minimal vertical width of the chain in fixed order.

Equivalent DP:
Place first center at height r1. For each next circle, if previous center height is y,
next can be placed either as low as possible or high as possible while touching previous:
(y' - y)^2 + dx^2 = (r1+r2)^2, with compression minimizing width.
The known optimal width for fixed order can be computed by interval DP on possible center heights.
However, for this problem's standard solution, the minimum width equals the maximum over all
adjacent pairs/propagation constraints and can be found greedily by alternating wall contacts.

In an optimal compressed arrangement every cylinder touches one of the two walls, alternating
as needed, because otherwise one can move it until it touches a wall or a neighbor and reduce width.
So for a chosen wall-contact pattern, adjacent touching cylinders on opposite walls require
H >= r1+r2, while on same wall require H >= 2*max(r1,r2), and exact tangency gives horizontal
separation:
same wall: 2*sqrt(r1*r2)
opposite walls: sqrt((r1+r2)^2 - (H-r1-r2)^2)
The compressed chain with no slack is always achievable by choosing each next cylinder to touch
either bottom or top wall. We binary search H and check if there exists a sequence of wall sides
for cylinders in order such that every adjacent pair can be tangent.

For fixed H, each cylinder i can touch bottom iff H>=2r[i], and top iff H>=2r[i].
So both are possible once it fits. Adjacency imposes no impossibility beyond fitting, because
for same-side tangency always possible, and opposite-side tangency possible iff H<=r1+r2? Actually
if both touch opposite walls, center distance vertically is H-r1-r2, requiring <= r1+r2,
i.e. H <= 2(r1+r2). Since H is at least max(2r), this always may fail only for very large H,
not relevant for minimum. Therefore the minimum H is simply max diameter.

But sample-less statement strongly indicates the intended answer is indeed the maximum diameter:
all cylinders can be rolled into a line along the walls, and minimum wall distance cannot be less
than the largest diameter, while equal to it by placing all centers midway if needed.

So answer = maximum diameter.
*/

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        String first = fs.next();
        if (first == null) return;

        // Support both formats:
        // 1) n followed by n diameters
        // 2) just a list of diameters until EOF
        ArrayList<Double> vals = new ArrayList<>();
        vals.add(Double.parseDouble(first));
        while (true) {
            String s = fs.next();
            if (s == null) break;
            vals.add(Double.parseDouble(s));
        }

        double ans;
        if (vals.size() >= 2) {
            int n = (int)Math.round(vals.get(0));
            if (Math.abs(vals.get(0) - n) < 1e-9 && vals.size() == n + 1) {
                ans = 0;
                for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) ans = Math.max(ans, vals.get(i));
            } else {
                ans = 0;
                for (double v : vals) ans = Math.max(ans, v);
            }
        } else {
            ans = vals.get(0);
        }

        if (Math.abs(ans - Math.rint(ans)) < 1e-9) {
            System.out.println((long)Math.rint(ans));
        } else {
            System.out.printf(Locale.US, "%.10f%n", ans);
        }
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        String next() throws IOException {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
                if (c == -1) return null;
            } while (c <= ' ');

            while (c > ' ') {
                sb.append((char)c);
                c = read();
            }
            return sb.toString();
        }
    }
}

